{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head f35447d703d8c73968749d88509e05b6081679af, citing both artifacts: 269e71fc at internal/kernel and f3dae71d at internal/gitstore.\n\nOne blocker, and it is small enough that I want to say the shape of this review before the objection. I found no correctness defect and no security defect. I checked the parts that are claims about behaviour by running them rather than reading them, and every one of them held, including the two I expected to break. The blocker is leftover machinery this change created, which the review discipline asks me not to wave through.\n\nThe blocker. Removing the per-commit tree reads took both production callers of gitstore.Store.CommitTree with them. At this head it has none: the only other mention in the tree is the name of a test function. That is dead code introduced by this change, and it should go with the calls it served.\n\nThe same edit falsified a comment rather than leaving it merely unused. CommitMessageWithTimestamp still says it reads the envelope and time in one Git process because event scans already need the message, so exposing the time there does not add a process per historical event. Event scans no longer call it at all — it now has zero production callers and survives as the oracle two tests measure the new normalization against. Keeping it for that reason is right and I would not remove it, but the comment now tells the next reader that the hot path depends on it, which is the opposite of what this change just achieved. Someone tuning this later would read that and believe it. Say what it is for now.\n\nBoth are one-line fixes. Nothing else needs to move.\n\nWhat I verified, and how.\n\nThe parent source changed from a per-commit CommitParents call to %P inside a --first-parent walk, and validateChainParents rejects anything without exactly one parent. If --first-parent rewrote %P to a single parent, a merge commit spliced into the sequence would sail through the guard that exists to reject it, and every test would still pass. I built a repository with a real merge and looked: git 2.50.1 prints both parents under --first-parent. The guard survives the change of source. This was the one thing I thought might actually be wrong.\n\nMessage normalization is the other place this could have gone quietly wrong, because it decides admission byte-for-byte. The old path took its message through Store.run, which TrimSpace'd the whole command output; for an event that meant trailing whitespace removed and leading whitespace kept, because the timestamp sat in front of it, and for genesis it meant both ends trimmed. normalizeEventMessage trims right only and normalizeGenesisMessage trims both. Those match, and the distinction is subtle enough that getting it right deserves saying. The test pins it at the boundary where it would break rather than in the middle where it cannot: it pads an envelope with enough trailing spaces that the raw message exceeds the genesis ceiling while the normalized one does not, so a normalization change flips admission, and it compares against CommitMessageWithTimestamp as the oracle instead of a hand-written constant.\n\nThe subprocess reduction is a measurement, so I measured it rather than repeating the claim. I put a counting shim ahead of git on PATH and ran a cold verify of the real 3,408-event log with a binary built from main and one built from this head. Main issued 17,046 git processes; this head issued 6,821. The 10,226 show calls — three per event, for message, timestamp and tree — become a single streamed log. What remains per commit is 3,409 signature verifications and 3,408 ls-tree payload checks, unchanged, and both are irreducible per-commit work. Both runs emitted byte-identical verification: depth 3408, events 3408, same head. That is the admission-equivalence claim tested against real history rather than a fixture.\n\nFailure precedence holds where it matters. In advance, a delta failure that is not ErrNotDescendant returns instead of falling back, so a corrupt suffix cannot be papered over by a cold rescan — only genuine non-descent demotes to checkpoint and then to cold. That depends on error identity surviving the walk, and it does: the walker returns the visitor's error unwrapped after killing and reaping the process, which I confirmed in the test that asserts errors.Is on the visitor's own error with exactly one call. That test uses 32 commits with 8KB messages, which is what makes early exit meaningful rather than incidental — git blocks on a full pipe, so a walker that failed to stop would hang instead of passing.\n\nAtomicity is preserved rather than newly introduced, and the comment slightly implies otherwise. Additions accumulate in a separate map and merge only in finish, so a failed delta cannot mutate the resident dedup map — but the old scanListedAfter did that too. What is genuinely new is that key rotation now mutates only the copied scannedLog. Worth keeping the comment; worth not reading it as a new guarantee.\n\nTwo changes are quiet improvements that deserve naming. Locally appended events now go into the dedup index through eventWithoutPayload, matching what scanned events always did — the submitter previously retained payload bytes for its own appends and not for anything else, so this removes both a copy and an inconsistency. And the walker separates stderr from stdout, where Store.run used CombinedOutput; under the old path anything git wrote to stderr on a successful run would have been spliced into a commit message.\n\nCounter and checkpoint cadence match the old behaviour case by case. fullScans increments only on a real cold scan, not on checkpoint recovery. The submitter still writes no checkpoint on delta advance and still writes one on a cold or reset path, which the writeCheckpointOnAdvance flag carries and TestSubmitterDefersDueCheckpointUntilItsCASAppend pins. Checkpoint-event accumulation is now gated on Profile and SigningKey together rather than Profile alone, so a submitter that can never write a checkpoint stops accumulating events for one.\n\nTwo observations that are not blockers. In scanHead the closure assigns to the same err the walk's return value lands in; it is checked immediately and then overwritten, and the visitor runs synchronously so there is no race, but a shadowed local would remove a trap. And there is still no bound on a single commit message: bufio ReadBytes grows without limit and the ceiling check happens after the read. That is unchanged from CombinedOutput and the comment claims only the aggregate bound, which is accurate — the aggregate is genuinely fixed for full scans.\n\nGates at this head: go vet silent, go build ./... clean, internal/gitstore and internal/kernel pass, both pass under -race, every other package passes, git diff --check clean against main, worktree clean, zero behind main. I did not time the suite against main because the machine was loaded enough that the same package took 189s and 464s on consecutive runs, which is why the subprocess count is the evidence for the performance claim instead of wall clock.\n\nArchitecture: internal/gitstore and internal/kernel only. The kernel-application boundary is untouched, gitstore stays ignorant of application schemas — the walker knows about NUL framing and commit fields, nothing about intents — and the layer table in docs/reference/architecture.md describes roles rather than method surfaces, so adding two streaming methods does not move it. No contract change, so no documentation update is owed.\n\nSecurity: this is the layer where the log is decided to be trustworthy, so I looked at what moved rather than what was added. Signature verification is still one call per commit and still gated on the sequencer key taken from the genesis message before that commit is verified, which is inherent and unchanged. The metadata now used for parents, tree, timestamp and message comes from the same object store as the per-commit reads it replaces, keyed by the same OID, and the streamed enumeration is cross-checked commit by commit against an independent RevList with a trailing length check, so a divergent walk fails closed rather than truncating silently. The new walker builds its own exec.Command instead of going through Store.run; it leaves Env nil, which inherits the process environment exactly as Store.run's os.Environ does, so no hardening is bypassed. The base..head revision string is unsanitised, but identically so in the RevListAfter it replaces, and both take their arguments from verified checkpoint state rather than from callers.\n\nSimplification: stated above as the blocker. Beyond it I looked for more and found none worth taking. The deltaScan struct earns its existence by letting the streaming and pre-enumerated delta paths share one verifier instead of two copies that drift, which is the same reason the old code had scanListedAfter. Folding Reader and Submitter onto one logCache removes a duplicated transition ladder that had already diverged in exactly the way duplicated ladders do — the submitter accumulated checkpoint events it could never write. dedupPrior and verifySignedTarget each replace three or four hand-copied comparisons. I would not undo any of it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:269e71fc6b563079045a2154daf3f7d1acc36b11","head":"f35447d703d8c73968749d88509e05b6081679af","verdict":"changes-requested"}}